Title: EP01 — Just Build It
Credit: Written by
Author: vibecode.town
Series: Vibe Coder Survival Guide Season 1
Source: Act 1-1 (I thought it worked) + Act 1-2 (Monday morning, everything broke)
Draft date: 2026-03-12
Contact: vibecode.town

# SEGMENT 1: HOOK [0:00-0:20]
# visual_type: sitcom
# characters: vee
# shorts_candidate: true

= Vee celebrates → explosion. Show the result first.

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT

Five monitors glow in a dark room. Vee leans back in her chair. Each screen shows code, dashboards, terminals — all running.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This is the moment every vibe coder lives for.

= Vee throws both arms up. Eyes wide. Huge grin. "BUILD SUCCESS" flashes green on every screen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You asked AI to build it. And it did. All of it.

= BEAT: 1 second of silence. One monitor flickers.

= One screen turns red. Then two. Then all five. Error messages cascade like a waterfall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then, three seconds later...

= Vee's smile disappears. She slowly lowers her mug.

= CU: Vee's face. Pure confusion. She can't read a single error.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
...it all falls apart.

> TITLE CARD: **JUST BUILD IT**

---

# SEGMENT 2: SITCOM ACT 1 [0:20-1:35]
# visual_type: sitcom
# characters: vee, bee
# shorts_candidate: false

= Three days earlier. Vee's vibe coding montage. Bee's first appearance.

INT. VEE'S ROOM - DAY (3 DAYS EARLIER)

> INSERT: "3 days earlier"

Bright room. Vee sits at her monitor. Terminal open.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three days ago, Vee discovered something magical.

= Vee types. Files auto-generate on screen. Folders, code, packages — appearing from nothing.

= Vee's eyes go wide. She jumps out of her chair.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You type what you want. AI builds it. Five minutes. Done.

= MONTAGE: Rapid cuts. Vee types → app appears. Types → app appears. Types → app appears. Each time faster. Each time her grin gets bigger.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A to-do app. Done.
A database. Done.
A login page. Done.

= Vee spins in her chair, arms crossed, nodding like a CEO.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She thought she could build anything.

= On the desk, next to the monitor — a small bee sits on a pencil cup. BEE. Wings folded. Eyes closed. Completely unimpressed.

= Vee doesn't notice. She keeps typing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But there was someone who'd seen this story before.

= Bee opens one eye. Stares at Vee. Holds up a tiny sign: "WHAT ARE YOU BUILDING?"

= Vee finally notices. Freezes. Stares at the sign. Blinks.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She couldn't answer.

= Bee flips the sign. The back reads: "EXACTLY."

= Bee folds the sign, tucks it under one wing, and flies away. No drama. Just gone.

---

# SEGMENT 3: EXPLAINER [1:35-3:15]
# visual_type: explainer
# characters: []
# shorts_candidate: true

= Transition to abstract world. "Spec = Blueprint" metaphor.

> TRANSITION: Camera zooms into a pencil on Vee's desk → pencil morphs into a blue line → blueprint world

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here's an empty lot.

= Wide, dark navy surface. Empty space in the center.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You want to build something here.

= Left side: a blueprint unfolds. Blue lines trace the outline of a building.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
With a blueprint, you start with the foundation.
Then the frame.
Then the walls.
There's an order. Each piece knows where it goes.

= Foundation → frame → walls → roof. Geometric building assembles in sequence. Clean. Stable.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Without a blueprint?

= Right side: colorful blocks drop from above, piling randomly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You just... stack.
And it goes up. It actually goes up.

= The pile grows taller. It starts leaning.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a while, it looks like it's working.

= The pile wobbles. Pauses.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then...

= Collapse. Blocks scatter everywhere.

= Back to the blueprint side. Clean building standing tall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A spec is a blueprint.
It's not a hundred-page document.
It's one sentence: "What am I building?"

= Text appears on the blueprint: "I want to access my home computer from anywhere."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You don't need to know every detail upfront.

= New scene. A small skateboard rolls into frame.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Start with a skateboard. Four wheels. It rolls. That's enough.

= Skateboard morphs into a bicycle. Bicycle morphs into a car.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You can always make it bigger later.
But you have to know what "it" is first.

= Text appears: "This is a skateboard." Simple. Clear.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That one line? That's where a spec starts.

> TRANSITION: Car shape zooms in → morphs into a toy car on Vee's desk → camera pulls back

---

# SEGMENT 4: SITCOM ACT 2 [3:15-4:30]
# visual_type: sitcom
# characters: vee, bee
# shorts_candidate: false

= Vee alone. Stops coding. Writes frustrations on paper. Discovers what she's building.

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT (PRESENT)

Five monitors. Red errors. Vee's hands hover over the keyboard.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Back in the present. Everything still broken.

= Vee's fingers tremble. She doesn't know what to type.

= She pulls her hands back. Pushes the chair away from the desk.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then she did something unusual.
She stopped coding.

= Vee opens a desk drawer. Pulls out paper and a pencil. Turns her back to the monitors.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She started writing. Not code. Just... what was bothering her.

= CU: Paper. Messy handwriting appears:
= "I don't know what I built."
= "There are five of them. They collect data. They organize. They analyze."

= The pencil stops.

= "But analyze WHAT?"

= Vee stares at the paper. One second.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And there it was. The question she'd been avoiding.

= The pencil moves again. Crosses out the old lines with big strokes.

= Writes fresh: "I want to use my home computer from anywhere."

= Below: "Files should stay in sync." "Access from any device." "Can't drop the connection."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Not features. Not tools.
The reason. The one sentence that explains everything.

= Vee picks up the paper. Turns back to the monitors.

= Red error messages. Before: an unreadable wall of text. Now —

= CU: One error line highlights itself: "Connection refused: sync agent cannot reach host."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The same errors. But now, one line makes sense.

= Vee's eyes track the line. Something clicks — shown through a slow blink, a slight tilt of the head.

= Corner of the frame: Bee sits on the edge of the desk. Watching. 0.5 seconds. Looks away.

---

# SEGMENT 5: ENDING [4:30-4:50]
# visual_type: sitcom
# characters: vee, bee
# shorts_candidate: true

= Mirror of HOOK. Same room, same errors, different person.

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT (CONTINUOUS)

= **Exact same composition as HOOK.** Five monitors. Red errors. Dark room.

Vee sits in her chair. The mug is in the same spot.

But Vee isn't panicking.

= She pins a piece of paper next to the monitor. It reads: "I want to use my home computer from anywhere."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Everything is still broken.

= BEAT.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But now she knows what to fix.

= Bee, corner of the frame. Tiny nod. 0.5 seconds. Back to deadpan.

> END CARD

NARRATOR (V.O.)
...at least, she thinks she does.

> NEXT: EP02 — "Fix One, Break Two"
